Pantheon: Cultural Visual Rosetta Stone + Prism Guitar

These belong together. The Cultural Visual Rosetta Stone translates meaning across visual traditions, while the Prism Guitar explores translating physical color relationships into sound, then building an entirely new musical culture around them.

The Rosetta Stone was previously an architectural proposal, not a functioning tool. I’ll make a first working prototype rather than leave it as another concept.

1. The Prism Guitar: seven strings, seven colors

Two legitimate ways to tune it

Spectral tuning — physics first. Start with representative wavelengths of red through violet. Convert light wavelength to frequency, then repeatedly halve that frequency until it reaches an audible pitch. This preserves the frequency ratios of the selected light wavelengths.

Modal tuning — music first. Assign the seven strings the notes C, D, E, F, G, A, B, each associated with one rainbow color. Use different tonal centers to explore the seven diatonic modes.

Neither mapping is biologically ordained. The visible spectrum is continuous, and human perception divides it into color categories; seven rainbow colors are one conventional classification. NASA describes the human visible range as approximately 380–700 nm.

The distinction matters creatively. Spectral tuning produces unusual intervals and microtonal relationships. Modal tuning makes conventional melodies, harmony, and compositional experiments much easier.

I would preserve both as separate modes rather than sacrifice one.

And I particularly like your biological suggestion: we can design the instrument around living-system principles—resonance, feedback, branching, sensitivity, adaptation, and even alternative robotic anatomies—rather than assuming it must behave like an ordinary human guitar.

